#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid "PEC with SLC for the latest version of Qiskit mitigation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "PEC with SLC"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<OpenInLabBanner notebookPath=\"addons/qiskit-mitigation/guides/pec-with-"
"slc.ipynb\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[Probabilistic error cancellation (PEC)](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1612.02058) is a technique for mitigating quantum noise in observable expectation value calculations. It lets you compute an unbiased estimate of the expectation values. This technique works by executing an ensemble of noisy circuits injected with the inverse of a learned noise model. The noise model is then approximated to a Pauli channel by using Pauli twirling (also known as [randomized compiling](https://journals.aps.org/pra/abstract/10.1103/PhysRevA.94.052325)). Such noise models can be learned efficiently and can be mitigated by using PEC (see [Probabilistic error cancellation with sparse Pauli-Lindblad models on noisy quantum\n"
"processors](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2201.09866) for more details).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"PEC requires exponential shot overhead to compute an unbiased estimate. "
"[Shaded Lightcones (SLC)](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.04401) is a technique "
"that uses classical resources to bound the sensitivity to errors throughout "
"the circuit. Using these bounds, the user can controllably tune the bias in "
"the observable estimation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Shaded Lightcones](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-"
"mitigation/pec_with_slc.svg)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This guide shows how to use PEC combined with SLC to mitigate gate noise "
"with reduced shot overhead compared to vanilla PEC.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The workflow follows:\n"
"1. Specify a circuit and observable.\n"
"2. Compute noisy, unmitigated expectation values.\n"
"3. Compute forward and backward bounds on the bias.\n"
"4. Learn the entangling layers' noise by using qiskit-noise-learning.\n"
"5. Use the learned noise to merge the computed bounds into a local scales matrix.\n"
"6. Choose the desired bias bound based on the shot overhead-bias trade-off.\n"
"7. Prepare a PEC + SLC program, execute the program, and post-process.\n"
"8. Compare the mitigated expectation values and shot overhead.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Specify a circuit and observable {#specify-a-circuit-and-observable}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Prepare a mirror circuit of a Trotterized time evolution of a 1D Ising chain"
" Hamiltonian on eight qubits. Mirror the circuit to cancel its unitary and "
"form an identity circuit, so each observable's ideal expectation value is "
"known to be 1 without the need to compute it. In addition, mirroring doubles"
" the circuit depth, which in turn doubles the noise's effect.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Use the angle $\\theta= \\frac{\\pi}{4}$ to make the dynamics non-Clifford.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-"
"mitigation/guides/pec-with-slc/extracted-"
"outputs/ef838b27-9c95-4425-9458-97dd1167d4ae-1.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Compute noisy, unmitigated expectation values {#compute-noisy-unmitigated-"
"expectation-values}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A [`QuantumProgram`](/guides/executor-input-output#inputs-quantum-programs) "
"contains a quantum circuit and a "
"[samplex](/addons/samplomatic/guides/samplex-io) in a format suitable for "
"the [Executor](/guides/get-started-with-executor) primitive. "
"`MitigationTask.prepare` builds a `QuantumProgram` from the circuit, the "
"observables and the parameter values, and boxes the circuit with the given "
"boxing options. Set the ``measure_annotations`` boxing option to "
"``\"change_basis\"`` to rotate the measurements into the required bases, and"
" add ``add_tags: \"unique_box\"`` so the simulator can map a noise model "
"onto the circuit's boxes. The program is Pauli twirled by default, which on "
"hardware turns coherent noise into the Pauli noise that the mitigation "
"methods assume. `num_randomizations` sets how many twirled instances of each"
" circuit are sampled and `shots_per_randomization` sets how many shots each "
"instance receives, so their product is the shot limit per circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If you have QPU access, you can execute the program with an `Executor` built"
" from a backend of the `QiskitRuntimeService`. This example uses an "
"`AerSimulator` to run the program locally. Its `layer_noise_model` simulator"
" option injects noise from a given Pauli-Lindblad noise model on every layer"
" of entangling gates. Here we generate a random Pauli-Lindblad noise model "
"with an error per layered gate (EPLG) of ``0.01``, almost an order of "
"magnitude higher than typical current Heron QPUs, to magnify the effect of "
"using SLC also on a small scale problem.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Compute forward and backward bounds on the bias {#compute-forward-and-"
"backward-bounds-on-the-bias}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Before spending any QPU time on noise learning, SLC uses classical "
"simulation to bound each potential error's contribution to the observable's "
"bias. This is done in two complementary passes:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Forward propagation**: each Pauli error is propagated forward through the "
"remainder of the circuit and its commutator with the observable is "
"evaluated. An error that anti-commutes with the observable is the only kind "
"that can bias the result, so this pass produces an upper bound on each "
"error's forward impact.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Backward propagation**: each error is propagated backward to the start of "
"the circuit and its commutator with the initial state is evaluated. Because "
"the circuit starts in the all-zero state, errors that commute with it cannot"
" affect the outcome, tightening the bound from the other direction.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The two bounds together form a tight bracket around every error's worst-case"
" contribution to the bias. In the next step these bounds are merged with the"
" learned noise rates to rank which errors are worth correcting and which can"
" be safely left alone. The "
"`inject_noise_strategy=\"individual_modification\"` boxing option is "
"required here so that each `InjectNoise` annotation in the boxed circuit has"
" a unique identity that the SLC bounds can be attached to.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For larger-scale problems, these computations may demand substantial "
"classical computational resources.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Learn the entangling layers' noise {#learn-the-entangling-layers-noise}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In order to compute a final estimation of how much each error impacts on the"
" expectation value's bias, as well as how expensive it would be to correct "
"it, we learn the noise rates and apply them to the predicted noise bounds we"
" computed before.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"PEC (as well as PEC+SLC) assumes a twirled noise model of each layer of "
"entangling gates in the circuit. If you assume a Markovian noise model, the "
"depth of the circuit is assumed to not affect the noise channel of a given "
"entangling layer. Therefore, only the noise model of each unique layer of "
"entangling gates should be learned. The circuit used in this guide can be "
"split into two unique layers. Designing circuits to have a minimal set of "
"unique entangling layers is important if you want to learn a noise model, "
"since the unique layers must be learned independently. If you have too many "
"unique layers in your circuit, noise learning can become infeasible due to "
"the sampling cost of learning many layers.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`qiskit-noise-learning` is used to learn the noise. The learner repeats each"
" layer _`fragment_depths`_ times in its learning circuits, twirls each "
"circuit _`num_randomizations`_ times, and fits the decay of the measured "
"fidelities to a Pauli-Lindblad model. These parameters can affect the "
"accuracy of the learned model. The more accurate the learned noise model is,"
" the more accurate the noise cancellation will be.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following code draws the learned noise model with the two layers merged "
"into one map. Each qubit is a pie of its weight-1 X, Y and Z rates summed "
"over the two layers, and each edge shows the learned weight-2 rates. For "
"example, a rate of ``3e-4`` means that error occurs on roughly three of "
"every 10,000 uses of the layer.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-"
"mitigation/guides/pec-with-slc/extracted-"
"outputs/c26591b8-f350-470f-bebc-3474f1d83bbf-0.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Use the learned noise to merge the computed bounds into local scales matrix "
"{#use-the-learned-noise-to-merge-the-computed-bounds-into-local-scales-"
"matrix}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"With both the classical bounds (from the previous step) and the learned "
"noise rates in hand, we can now compute a *local scales matrix* — a per-"
"error weight that determines how aggressively PEC should cancel each "
"individual noise term.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The key idea of SLC is that not all errors need to be fully cancelled. An "
"error whose forward and backward bounds are both small has a negligible "
"effect on the observable regardless of its rate, so it can be left partially"
" or fully un-mitigated. `merge_bounds` merges the classical bounds with the "
"learned Pauli-Lindblad rates, than `compute_local_scales` assigns each error"
" a scale between 0 (completely ignored) and 1 (fully cancelled), subject to "
"a user-specified `bias_tolerance`. Errors with a large estimated bias "
"contribution receive a scale close to 1 (full PEC treatment), while errors "
"with a small contribution receive a lower scale, reducing the sampling "
"overhead they carry.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"SLC exposes a continuous knob between the two extremes — vanilla PEC (zero "
"bias, highest overhead) and no mitigation at all (zero overhead, full noise "
"bias). The `bias_tolerance` parameter controls this knob: setting it to `0` "
"recovers full PEC, while larger values allow more residual bias in exchange "
"for a lower sampling overhead γ².\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This step sweeps `bias_tolerance` across a range of values and computes the "
"resulting sampling overhead for each. Plotting these points against the "
"full-PEC overhead helps you choose a working point that fits your shot "
"budget. For example, tolerating a 5 % residual bias may reduce the sampling "
"overhead by an order of magnitude compared with full PEC, making the "
"computation feasible on today's QPUs where it would otherwise not be.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/addons/qiskit-"
"mitigation/guides/pec-with-slc/extracted-"
"outputs/c4d1997b-0aa8-4234-baeb-f16a8c89be00-2.avif)\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Choose desired bias bound based on shot overhead-bias trade-off {#choose-"
"desired-bias-bound-based-on-shot-overhead-bias-trade-off}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"In this guide we choose a bias tolerance of 5%. Note that this bias "
"calculation is based on the learned noise model, which might not be "
"perfectly aligned with the real noise of the device.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Prepare a PEC + SLC program, execute the program, and post-process "
"{#prepare-a-pec-slc-program-execute-the-program-and-post-process}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"With the local scales chosen, we can build the full PEC+SLC execution "
"program. The `PEC.prepare` call constructs a `QuantumProgram` that encodes "
"the anti-noise injection strategy into the circuit's box annotations. "
"Passing `noise_gain=\"slc_local_scales\"` instructs the executor to use the "
"per-error weights set by `set_slc_local_scales`, rather than applying the "
"same full inverse-noise gain to every error as vanilla PEC would.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"To produce a fair comparison, a second `PEC` task is prepared with "
"`noise_gain=0`, which corresponds to full PEC cancellation with no SLC bias "
"allowance. Both programs are added to the same `QuantumProgram` so they "
"share a single execution job — their circuits are interleaved and executed "
"together, meaning any hardware drift affects both equally.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"After the job completes, `postprocess` converts the raw measurement "
"bitstrings into expectation values, applying the appropriate PEC rescaling "
"factor γ for each task.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Compare the mitigated expectation values and shot overhead {#compare-the-"
"mitigated-expectation-values-and-shot-overhead}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The ideal expectation value of the mirrored circuit is 1 by construction, so"
" the bias of any estimate is simply `|1 - result|`. This final cell prints "
"the bias and total shot count for three scenarios side-by-side:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Unmitigated**: the raw noisy expectation value with no error cancellation "
"applied.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Full PEC**: zero-bias error cancellation using the complete inverse noise "
"model on every layer, at the full γ² sampling overhead.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**PEC+SLC**: error cancellation guided by the shaded-lightcone local scales,"
" tolerating the chosen `bias_tolerance` in exchange for a substantially "
"lower shot count.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Comparing the three rows shows the concrete benefit of SLC: the mitigated "
"bias of PEC+SLC stays well below the unmitigated bias while the total number"
" of shots is significantly smaller than what full PEC required.\n"
msgstr ""
